If we attempt to evaluate this limit directly, we get an indeterminate form:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{x \to  π/4}\frac{\sin^{2} x - \cos^{2} x}{2\sin x\cos 2x} &= (\sin^{2} \left(\frac{π}{4}\right) - \cos^{2} \left(\frac{π}{4}\right))/(2\sin \left(\frac{π}{4}\right)\cos (2 \cdot \frac{π}{4})) \\
&= (1 - 1)/(\sqrt{2}\cos \left(\frac{π}{2}\right)) \\
&= \frac{0}{0}
\end{aligned}
$$

So we need to simplify the expression. The double angle formula for cosine will be useful in this case:

$$
\cos{2x}=\cos^2{x}-\sin^2{x}
$$

Using the double angle formula above, we have

$$
\begin{aligned}
\lim_{x \to  π/4}\frac{\sin^{2} x - \cos^{2} x}{2\sin x\cos 2x} &= \lim_{x \to  π/4}(\sin^{2} x - \cos^{2} x)/(2\sin x(\cos^{2} x - \sin^{2} x)) \\
&= \lim_{x \to  π/4}(- (\cos^{2} x - \sin^{2} x))/(2\sin x(\cos^{2} x - \sin^{2} x)) \\
&= \lim_{x \to  π/4}\left(-\frac{1}{2\sin x}\right) \\
&=-\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\
&=-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

